{
  "about": "The markdown part's Markdown, as every renderer reads it, into blocks and their spans. Blocks: a line `#` to `######` and a space is a `heading` of that `level`; a line `-`, `*` or `+` and a space, or a number with `.` or `)` and a space, is an `item` (`ordered` with its `number` for a number), whose `depth` is its leading spaces halved, at most 3; a fence of three backticks starts a `code` block, its lines as written up to the closing fence; other lines are a `paragraph`. Lines that follow a paragraph or an item without a blank line join it, with one space. A blank line ends a paragraph or an item. Spans: the text of a block split where its look changes, merged where it does not; a span lists only what holds for it (`bold`, `italic`, `code`, `link`). Inline: a backslash takes the next punctuation as written; `code` between backticks is as written; `**` or `__` around text is bold, `*` or `_` around text is italic (a `_` inside a word is a letter); a delimiter followed by a space, or never closed, is a letter. `[text](address)` is a link when the address starts with `http://`, `https://` or `mailto:`; with another address only its text shows. HTML is text.",
  "cases": [
